A||||8||skitime||How to modify a defrost switch.||||1||
Z||000000||skitime||11-29-2001||05:54 PM||||In the following thread there was a discussion about using the defrost switch to control other devices. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015857.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015857.html</A>  <BR>Since the switch is a momentary switch it was discussed how difficult it is.  I suggested to modify the switch like I had done. After explaining how to do the modification, theogre said "Modification of the switch won't be fun and may not even be possible w/o somehow damaging the switch."  Well I would like to pictorially explain how simple it is and only takes two minutes.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pb290002.jpg">  <BR>Gently pry the rocker here.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pb290005.jpg">  <BR>Note spring in the center. Grab spring and pull it out.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pb290007.jpg">  <BR>Remove spring.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pb290008.jpg">  <BR>Put rocker back on.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pb290009.jpg">  <BR>Switch now functions like a single pole double throw switch.<P>I use this switch for my driving lights in my chin spoiler. The bright light lets me know when it is on and it also lights dimly when the parking lights are on.  I eventually want to use the second switch position to operate my remote garage door opener so that can be put out of sight.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 11-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cozmo||11-29-2001||06:34 PM||||Good stuff!<BR>Now, how can we make these switches look better?<BR>Mine is chalky white fungus lookin'  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR><B>Bob Cardone</B><BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/thankssig.jpg">   <BR><B>'85 2M6 SE Targa</B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-29-2001||08:01 PM||||Good pictures. <P>I just said it wouldn't be fun. I've worked on the switches and find it rather annoying. Those are close in construction to the window switch but wired for the lights. I've had to recondition power window switches a bunch of times. It's a nasty little job but has to be done when you can't get new ones when you need 'em.....<P>The reason I said they might be damaged is that the plastics can be really brittle on old switches.<P>As for cleaning... Try a buffer on a variable speed dremel type tool. try it at low speed and practice on a scrap one first. Hold the buffer too long or spin it too fast and you can ruin the plastic really fast.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||extinct||11-29-2001||08:59 PM||||Thanks for the pics skitime!  Now if I wanted to use the switch for a manual radiator fan switch how would I wire it so that the light would come on when the switch was pressed to activate the fan?  The online service guide has you add a ground wire to a switch, but what about power for the little light?  <P>Thanks once a gain.  You have really helped out!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||skitime||11-29-2001||09:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by extinct:<BR><B>Thanks for the pics skitime!  Now if I wanted to use the switch for a manual radiator fan switch how would I wire it so that the light would come on when the switch was pressed to activate the fan?  The online service guide has you add a ground wire to a switch, but what about power for the little light?  <P>Thanks once again.  You have really helped out!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Extinct here is the schematic for the rear defogger.  What I did was replaced the solid state timer relay with a standard relay after modifying the switch. I connected everything else as shown except: 1.I changed the pink and black wire connecting to the gauge fuse to the parking light circuit so my driving lights would go out it I turn off the parking or headlights. 2. I connected the purple/white wire which went to the rear defogger to my driving lights.  You would have the connect the purple/white wire to you fan.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Defogger.jpg"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||extinct||11-29-2001||11:26 PM||||One last question:  If the car did not come with the rear defog, would I even have the<BR>time delay relay?||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||11-30-2001||07:05 AM||||No you will need to get the wiring harness for the defroster for a car in the yard.  That is what I did.  It is a seperate harness from the main wiring harness.||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||11-30-2001||01:23 PM||||I have my driving lights connected to a defogger switch, but it functions just the same as it would it my car had been equipped with the defogger window.  I dont have a timer relay or the wiring harness or anything.  The switch still has the spring in it so circuits are open in rest.<P>The latching relay circuit I constructed uses 3 relays that will hold them selves closed when they are inititated with a short pulse (ON motion of switch).  They will then turn on my driving lights and the indicator light in the switch.  Pressing the switch to off un-latches the relays.  The panel light illuminates from a feed from my trunk release switch so it dims too.  I just want to find out a way to put a different icon in there with the fog light symbol instead of defogger window.<P>I did have to modify the switch in order to get the indicator light to come on. If you look really closely at the two of the terminals (ones that are on top, and bottom, and not a terminal in between) these are the terminals for the panel light and the indicator light.  My relay circuit wouldnt work with the way the switch was wired for these, becuase they share a ground or +12V (dont know which one, been a while) feed for the (stock) timer circuit.  So, I had to un solder the itty bitty 28 gauge (or thinner) wires so I could provide a +12v feed for the indicator light to come on without resetting my timer circuit.<P>That description is probably very confusing, but if you look at your switch you'll see the little wires that I am talking about.  Of course, hindsight is 20/20, and I would have probably done mine your way skitime if I would have thought about it.  One thing though, dosent the switch now have a propensity just to fall back to rest?  There isnt really any mechanical system in the switch holding it in the closed position, is there?||||reg||1
Z||000008||skitime||11-30-2001||02:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>Of course, hindsight is 20/20, and I would have probably done mine your way skitime if I would have thought about it.  One thing though, dosent the switch now have a propensity just to fall back to rest?  There isnt really any mechanical system in the switch holding it in the closed position, is there?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, the switch feels like any normal on/off switch and does not fall back or feel loose. Actually the spring that was removed was to override the the springs in the contacts.   <P>||||reg||1
